    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense, but i'm having a hard time visualizing it. could u maybe walk me through a more detailed breakdown of the airflow? like, if u were describing it to someone who isn't a math person, how would u explain the way the air actually interacts with the shape of the wing to create that pressure difference?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Of course, let me simplify it. Imagine the wing as a giant wing-shaped scoop that's moving through the air. As the wing glides through, the top surface needs to catch more air to keep moving smoothly alongside the bottom. This is where Bernoulli's principle kicks in: the air rushes over the top faster than underneath the wing because the wing forces more air into a smaller space above it. This speed increase causes a drop in air pressure – just like sucking your thumb through a straw makes your drink come rushing up faster. The side of the wing that is under the flap, flying under it, is kind of more like a sliver of flat air trapped that moves creeping along with the wing but just enough to create an upward nudge to the wing. That gentle sag is Newton’s third law coming into play – for every push of air downwards, there's a push in the lift upwards. Thus, that clever wing shape we know makes planes soar, all thanks to Bernoulli and Newton teaming up.",
      "source": "target",
